WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:04.540
Over the years, Batman has more than earned
his place in pop culture legend — and for

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:06.319
more reasons than one.

00:00:06.319 --> 00:00:10.490
He boasts a remarkable rogues' gallery, he's
a key player in super-teams like the Justice

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:12.889
League, and he's a merchandising juggernaut
to boot.

00:00:12.889 --> 00:00:17.020
And of course, you can't ignore arguably the
most important aspects of Batman's iconic

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:18.680
appeal: the Batsuit.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:22.790
As long as there's been a Batman, there's
been a Batsuit in some form or fashion.

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:24.540
Some are cloth, and some are rubber.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.660
Some are armored, and some are lightweight.

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:31.210
They come in a range of color schemes, ear
and cape lengths, and utility belt sizes.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:35.199
Regardless of the specifics, though, Batman
rarely leaves the Batcave without one.

00:00:35.199 --> 00:00:38.450
His suits go a long way in keeping him safe,
and they help strike fear into the hearts

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:39.659
of Gotham City's criminals.

00:00:39.659 --> 00:00:41.570
"What gives you the right?

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:44.159
What's the difference between you and me?"

00:00:44.159 --> 00:00:46.940
"I'm not wearing hockey pads."

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:52.149
Still, as badass as many of Batman's costumes
may be, the truth is they're far from perfect

00:00:52.149 --> 00:00:55.929
— and if you place them into a real-world
context, some serious issues begin to present

00:00:55.929 --> 00:00:57.440
themselves.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.789
Arguably one of the most glaring problems
has even been examined by physicists, who

00:01:00.789 --> 00:01:05.160
explained why a Batsuit wouldn't work in real
life as it does in the comics, cartoons, or

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:06.160
movies.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:09.430
Any Batman fan will tell you that most Batsuits
are incomplete without a cape.

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:12.500
After all, they don't call him the "Caped
Crusader" for nothing.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:16.229
On the surface, the cape seems like little
more than an aesthetic choice, but in truth,

00:01:16.229 --> 00:01:19.670
many Batman incarnations have used it for
much more than looking good.

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:23.920
Aside from using it as a means of intimidation
against Gotham criminals, it has often been

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:27.079
used as a method of gliding across large distances.

00:01:27.079 --> 00:01:30.799
And while this may look cool on the silver
screen or in the pages of a comic book, the

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:34.539
truth is that this function isn't nearly as
plausible in reality.

00:01:34.539 --> 00:01:38.610
Back in 2012, physicists at the University
of Leicester published a study in the Journal

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:40.280
of Special Physics Topics.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:41.280
It read:

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:45.340
"Clearly gliding using a Batcape is not a
safe way to travel, unless a method to rapidly

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:47.729
slow down is used, such as a parachute."

00:01:47.729 --> 00:01:53.399
They noted that, after leaping from a 150-meter-high
Gotham City skyscraper, Batman could realistically

00:01:53.399 --> 00:01:59.679
glide for approximately 350 meters on a 4.7-meter
wingspan before the velocity levels off at

00:01:59.679 --> 00:02:00.789
110 kilometers per hour.

00:02:00.789 --> 00:02:05.670
At that point, an 80 kilometer per hour descent
would begin — causing the DC Comics mainstay

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:09.310
to plummet to his death, unless he found something
to break his fall.

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:12.670
Still, there's nothing wrong with pretending
that Batman can use his cape to glide from

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:13.790
building to building.

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:17.440
It's a fun mechanic to use in the Batman:
Arkham video games, for example, and it sets

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:19.260
up some neat visuals on the big screen.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:23.909
But there's one other aspect of the Batsuit
that’s even less practical than the cape:

00:02:23.909 --> 00:02:24.909
the cowl.

00:02:24.909 --> 00:02:28.590
Specifically, those worn by the likes of Michael
Keaton and his successors for their live-action

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:29.700
movie appearances.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:33.621
Seeing as these specific cowls consisted of
one solid piece from the shoulders up, it

00:02:33.621 --> 00:02:36.310
would be incredibly difficult for Batman to
turn his head.

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:40.860
In fact, this has even caused problems during
filming on Batman movies in the past.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:45.239
At a 2014 press event, Michael Keaton discussed
his struggles with the headpiece while filming

00:02:45.239 --> 00:02:50.150
1989's Batman, revealing that moving his head
at the neck was next to impossible.

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:53.659
As a result, he had no choice but to move
his entire upper body for moments in which

00:02:53.659 --> 00:02:56.269
Batman had to turn and look in a different
direction.

00:02:56.269 --> 00:02:59.780
That's obviously inconvenient for an actor,
let alone someone trying to fight off two

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:01.790
or three bad guys at once.

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:06.330
From cape to cowl, the Batsuit is something
of a mess by real-world standards, yet it's

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:10.580
typically presented as the ultimate suit of
armor at the cinema, on TV, and in the pages

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:12.200
of DC Comics.

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:15.489
At the end of the day, though, suspension
of disbelief is the name of the game with

00:03:15.489 --> 00:03:16.769
stuff like this.

00:03:16.769 --> 00:03:19.970
As long as it looks cool, that's all that
really matters.

